      "kind": "dossier optimality/uniqueness proof",
      "statement": "With both shapes free, a finite MLE exists for every nonconstant sample in $(0,1)$, and it is unique. Constant data have unbounded likelihood. Holding either shape gives a unique finite MLE even for constant interior data. Holding both leaves only likelihood evaluation.",
      "reason": "Put $t_i=-\\log x_i$ and $S(a)=\\sum_i\\log(1-e^{-at_i})$. Profiling gives $\\hat b=-n/S(a)$. As $a\\to0$ the profile behaves like $-n\\log|\\log a|+O(1)$. As $a\\to\\infty$ it is $a(n\\min_i t_i-\\sum_i t_i)+n\\log a+O(1)$, which tends to $-\\infty$ for nonconstant data and to $+\\infty$ for constant data. Hence the continuous profile attains a maximum in the nonconstant case. Strict concavity of this profile, proved below, makes the maximum unique. With $a$ held the likelihood is strictly concave in $b$. With $b$ held the score in $a$ strictly decreases from $+\\infty$ to $-\\sum_i t_i<0$. For $b<1$ its derivative is at most $-nb/a^2<0$, using $(at_i)^2e^{at_i}/(e^{at_i}-1)^2\\le1$."
    },
